# Gödel numbering

A **Gödel numbering** is an effective assignment of a natural number to every
formula and every proof of a formal theory, so that statements about formulas
and proofs become statements about numbers. It is the step that lets
arithmetic, a theory about numbers, carry a description of itself. In this
vault it is the one place where a [[level-crossing]] is carried out by an
explicit, checkable construction.

## Description

The discussion arrived here from a demand for precision: the only strange loop
that is "precise and mathematical" is the one built from Gödel numerals and
the incompleteness proof, so the definition should be reproduced with the
rigour of a set theory textbook. Gödel numbering is the first of the steps to
reproduce.

The construction runs in three moves.

1. **Code the syntax.** Fix a consistent, computably axiomatizable theory $T$
   extending Robinson arithmetic $Q$. Give each symbol a number, and each
   finite string of symbols a number computed from those. The code of a
   formula $\varphi$ is written $\ulcorner\varphi\urcorner$. The coding must
   be effective: from a number one can compute which string it codes, if any.
2. **Arithmetize the operations.** Under the coding, syntactic operations
   such as substituting a term for a variable become computable functions on
   numbers, and "$p$ codes a proof in $T$ of the formula coded by $x$" becomes
   a computable relation on numbers.
3. **Represent them inside the theory.** Every computable relation is
   representable in $Q$. So there is a formula $\operatorname{Proof}_T(p,x)$
   of arithmetic that $T$ itself can use, and from it the provability
   predicate

$$\operatorname{Prov}_T(x):=\exists p\,\operatorname{Proof}_T(p,x).$$

After the third move the hierarchy has been folded. The discussion drew it as
four stages.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 A[Arithmetic talks about numbers] --> B[Numbers encode arithmetic formulas]
 B --> C[Arithmetic represents proof relations]
 C --> D[Sentence expresses property of its own code]
 D -.self-reference.-> A
```

Gödel numbering supplies the first three stages. The fourth needs the
[[diagonal-lemma]].

Two points from the discussion fix what the numbering is and is not.

- **It is a representation, not yet a self-reference.** In the discussion's
  table of knowledge transformations it sits in the class "representation",
  next to the Fourier transform: an encoding of objects in a new language.
  An encoding by itself says nothing about itself. Self-reference appears
  only when a fixed point is taken over it.
- **Running it is not the same as the theory representing it.** A program can
  compute Gödel numbers, substitute and check proofs. That executes the
  construction from outside. The theorem needs the further fact that the
  theory represents those computations internally. The proposed experiment in
  HQL has to keep the two apart.

The choice of coding does not matter, provided it is effective. Gödel's own
used products of prime powers; any computable, decodable scheme gives the same
theorems.

## Relations

- It is the explicit mechanism of a [[level-crossing]] {relation: example-of}: object-level numbers come to stand for meta-level syntax.
- It collapses the separation described in [[object-and-meta-level]] {relation: uses} without abolishing it.
- It is the input to the [[diagonal-lemma]] {relation: part-of}, which adds the fixed point.
- It makes the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: part-of} expressible at all.
- It is the encoding component of a [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: part-of}.
- It defines the [[provability-predicate]] {relation: uses}, the formula through which a theory speaks of its own proofs.
- It is not yet [[self-reference]] {relation: distinguished-from}: an encoding refers to syntax in general, not to itself.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: a card that is retrieved as data by a cell on the same card is the vault's counterpart of a formula and its code.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[diagonal-lemma]].** For every property of codes there is a sentence
  provably equivalent to the claim that its own code has the property. It
  turns the encoding into self-reference.
- **[[godel-sentence]].** The fixed point of "is not provable". It is what the
  numbering was built to make possible.
- **[[reflective-formal-system]].** The discussion's schematic for a theory
  that carries an encoding of its own syntax and proofs. The numbering is its
  third component.
- **[[level-crossing]].** Treating something on one level of description as
  belonging to another. Elsewhere it is an impression; here it is a function.

